  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct evidence – Hop 1**  \nThe review of HBV‑associated HCC states that “recurrent liver inflammation caused by host immune responses during chronic HBV infection can lead to liver fibrosis and cirrhosis” [PMID: 29052129]. This links chronic HBV infection directly to sustained hepatic inflammation in humans.\n\n**Direct evidence – Hop 2**  \nA separate review of HBV‑associated hepatocarcinogenesis reports that “the predominant carcinogenic mechanisms … are chronic inflammation and the effects of cytokines in the development of fibrosis and liver cell proliferation” [PMID: 20485626]. This demonstrates that chronic liver inflammation promotes HCC development in humans.\n\n**Association evidence**  \nEpidemiologically, chronic HBV infection accounts for at least 50 % of worldwide HCC cases [PMID: 29052129], underscoring a strong link between the infection and the cancer, but without specifying the inflammatory intermediary.\n\n**Testable hypothesis**  \nIf chronic inflammation is a necessary intermediate, then therapeutic strategies that suppress HBV‑driven hepatic inflammation (e.g., anti‑cytokine agents) should lower the incidence of HCC in chronically infected patients; this can be evaluated in prospective antiviral‑plus‑anti‑inflammatory trials.\n\n**Graph‑derived candidate insights**  \n- c37d8f7746362201 GRAPH‑CONFIRMED source HBV infection causes chronic liver inflammation promotes HCC target [PMID: 29052129, 20485626].\n\n**Conclusion**  \nTogether, human‑based mechanistic reports provide direct evidence for each step—HBV infection causing chronic liver inflammation and that inflammation driving HCC—supporting the inferred pathogenic pathway."
  },
